  "summary": "Screwworm reaches Sonora days before a cattle-export crossing is due to reopen. Mexico's animal-health agency listed Sonora's first active case in Álamos. The US Agriculture Department has scheduled the Douglas, Arizona, cattle crossing to reopen on August 24, subject to Mexico following the joint control plan. Mexico will buy US drones and anti-drone systems for security operations. The United States announced that Mexico will be able to buy the equipment at competitive prices.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "Screwworm reaches Sonora days before a cattle-export crossing is due to reopen",
    "context": "Mexico's animal-health agency listed Sonora's first active case in Álamos. The US Agriculture Department has scheduled the Douglas, Arizona, cattle crossing to reopen on August 24, subject to Mexico following the joint control plan.",
    "background": "New World screwworm larvae feed on living tissue. The US Agriculture Department has tied the border reopening to the joint control plan and inspection of every animal entering through Douglas.",
    "view": "One confirmed case does not by itself cancel the reopening. Sonora had been one of the northern states kept free of the pest, so the case tests whether the control plan can contain an isolated detection without another trade closure.",
    "prediction": "Watch the US Agriculture Department's import page and Mexico's animal-health updates before August 24. If the crossing reopens on schedule with the case contained, that would support the control plan. A delay or more northern cases would show the trade risk is rising.",
    "analysisV": 9,
